The set V, on the other hand, is simply a collection of all sets, without any self-referential constraints. It includes all possible sets, including those that contain themselves as elements. In mathematical notation, we can represent this as:

Ω = {x | x is a set that does not contain x as an element}
V = {x | x is a set}

In other words, V is a more inclusive set that encompasses all possible sets, whereas Ω is a more restrictive set that excludes sets that contain themselves as elements.

